{"name":"Glitter Is Not Gold","hex":"#fedc57","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"glitter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bright, sparkling light; shininess or brilliance.","A shiny, decorative adornment, sometimes sprinkled on glue to make simple artwork."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To sparkle with light; to shine with a brilliant and broken light or showy luster; to gleam.","To be showy, specious, or striking, and hence attractive."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glitter"},{"term":"not","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Nomatsiguenga."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Negates the meaning of the modified verb.","To no degree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/not"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glitter","blocks":[{"text":"Glitter is an assortment of flat, small, reflective particles that are precision cut and come in a variety of shapes, sizes, and colors. Glitter particles resemble confetti, sparkles and sequins, but are somewhat smaller."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Glitter_close_up.jpg/330px-Glitter_close_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glitter"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Glitter_close_up.jpg/500px-Glitter_close_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Glitter_close_up.jpg/500px-Glitter_close_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Close-up of holographic glitter","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGlitter_close_up.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Mica_3157.jpg/500px-Mica_3157.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Mica_3157.jpg/500px-Mica_3157.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mica","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMica_3157.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Glitter_nail_polish_under_the_microscope%2C_magnification_x100.jpg/500px-Glitter_nail_polish_under_the_microscope%2C_magnification_x100.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Glitter_nail_polish_under_the_microscope%2C_magnification_x100.jpg/1280px-Glitter_nail